API = a way for software to communicate.
MCP = a standardized way for AI models/agents to use tools and data.

| Feature           | API                               | MCP                             |
| ----------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Full form         | Application Programming Interface | Model Context Protocol          |
| Main purpose      | Connect software/services         | Connect AI models to tools/data |
| Used by           | Any application                   | Mainly AI agents/LLMs           |
| Standardization   | Each API has its own design       | MCP provides a common protocol  |
| Example           | REST API, Gemini API              | MCP server exposing tools       |
| AI tool discovery | Usually you implement it yourself | Built into the MCP approach     |
| Communication     | App ↔ Service                     | AI ↔ MCP server ↔ Tool/Service  |

Using an API
Your App
   ↓
Weather API
   ↓
Weather Service


Using MCP
AI Agent
   ↓
MCP
   ↓
Weather MCP Server
   ↓
Weather API


The important difference

Think of API as the actual door to a service.

Think of MCP as a standardized adapter/interface that lets an AI agent discover and use those doors as tools.

For example:

             API
AI ───────────────────→ Google/GitHub/Database

             MCP
AI ──→ MCP Server ──→ API/Database/File/System
MCP doesn't replace APIs. An MCP server can actually use APIs internally.


for openclaw:
OpenClaw Agent
      ↓
     MCP
      ↓
   MCP Server
      ↓
 APIs / Databases / Files / External Tools

"APIs allow applications to communicate with services, but MCP provides a standardized interface specifically for AI agents to discover and use tools, resources, and external context. MCP can also sit on top of existing APIs rather than replacing them."







